sPaineBand', 'statuses_count': 64937, 'translator_type': 'none', 'url': 'https://t.co/ulWrUekUqq', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}, 'professional': {'rest_id': '1691604872750133575', 'professional_type': 'Creator', 'category': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1780395828323963199'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1713318018000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '66105', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="https://mobile.twitter.com" rel="nofollow">Twitter Web App</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE3ODAzOTU4MjgxMTAwNjE1Njg=', 'text': 'Our debut single Exile  is a song about how COVID truth tellers were abused throughout the pandemic. \n\nWe asked Grok to describe the meaning of the lyrics of Exile...\n\nThe song "Exile" by Thomas Paine Band is a commentary on the COVID-19 pandemic and the societal response to it. The lyrics express the experience of being ostracized for questioning the mainstream narrative and the actions taken by health organizations and government bodies.\n\nThe first verse refers to a time before the pandemic when people were "benign" and didn\'t pose a clear danger to each other. The narrator reflects on how they "see clearly" now that they are on the outside looking in, suggesting a shift in perspective due to the events of the pandemic.\n\nThe second verse describes how the narrator has learned from the situation but feels they haven\'t changed, while others have been "programmed" and "turned into the enemy." This could be interpreted as a commentary on the polarization of society during the pandemic, with those questioning the mainstream narrative being branded as the enemy.\n\nThe chorus emphasizes the theme of exile, with the narrator feeling banished from society for their views.\n\nThe third verse mentions the "Kool-aid," a reference to the Jonestown Massacre, implying that many people blindly followed the advice of health organizations and government bodies without question. The narrator contrasts this with the "red pilled," a term often used to describe those who have seen through a perceived deception or illusion, suggesting a divide between those who blindly follow and those who question.\n\nThe fourth verse discusses the narrator\'s experience as an outsider in their own homeland, unable to communicate effectively with those who have accepted the mainstream narrative. They accuse those who are "woke" of selling hate, suggesting that the division and ostracization they have experienced is driven by hostility rather than a genuine desire for understanding.\n\nThe bridge refers to the use of censorship to suppress dissenting voices, arguing that truth will eventually come to light despite attempts to conceal it.\n\nThe final verse suggests that the number of exiles is growing as more people wake up to the truth. It predicts a "reckoning" when the pendulum swings, implying a potential shift in public opinion or a change in the balance of power.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': [{'from_index': 17, 'to_index': 24, 'richtext_types': ['Bold', 'Italic']}]}, 'media': {'inline_media': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 17, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Wed Apr 17 00:40:18 +0000 2024', 'conversation_id_str': '1780395828323963199', 'display_text_range': [0, 279], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'media': [{'display_url': 'pic.x.com/OiUPdmSlul', 'expanded_url': 'https://x.com/ThomasPaineBand/status/1780395828323963199/video/1', 'id_str': '1780394729907073024', 'indices': [280, 303], 'media_key': '7_1780394729907073024', 'media_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/ext_tw_video_thumb/1780394729907073024/pu/img/VhkFMCPXm8qxggMc.jpg', 'type': 'video', 'url': 'https://t.co/OiUPdmSlul', 'additional_media_info': 